# APK Analysis 2 - From Forced Intent Calls to Resetting a Password

Last post I went through the four Manifest components and made the point that `exported="true"` on its own isn't the problem — what actually matters is what the component does with the Intent it receives. I left it there as a conclusion, but this time I wanted to actually build the other half and check it for real. Knowing it in theory just means I'd be second-guessing myself the next time it comes up in an actual assessment, so I built a test app and walked the whole chain: forced-call a exposed Intent, chain it through a redirect, and end up taking over the account.

New thing I learned today: the `am` (Activity Manager) command. Up until now I'd been testing by tapping through the UI by hand, but with `am start` you can jump straight into a component with no entry point on screen at all, as long as you know its name. Meaning: if a component is exported, it doesn't matter that there's no UI path to it — it's reachable regardless. This post is a record of learning `am`, finding an exposed Intent, and chaining it into a full attack.

## 1. Confirm normal login first

![1](/assets/img/posts/2026-08-10/1.png)

Baseline step. Tried logging in with `admin` / `1234`, and got "wrong password" as expected. Skipping this and jumping straight to the bypass invites the question later of whether normal auth was broken to begin with, so I always check it first. Confirms the normal logic works fine on its own.

## 2. Find the exposed Intent in the Manifest

![2](/assets/img/posts/2026-08-10/2.png)

Opened the source and checked the Manifest. Screens that should only be reachable post-login — `BoardActivity`, `ChangePasswordActivity` — are all `exported="false"`, but `QRConnectActivity` alone is `exported="true"`. There's a comment next to it saying it's an external QR proxy screen, "not a vulnerability bridge" — which is a little ironic, since what actually matters is how the input inside gets handled, not a comment declaring it safe. Attack surface identified.

## 3. Confirm it's actually forced-callable

![3](/assets/img/posts/2026-08-10/3.png)

Knew in theory that exported means externally callable, but wanted to see it directly, so I fired it:

```
adb shell "am start -n com.sosung.test_intent/.QRConnectActivity"
```

Result: `QRConnectActivity`, with zero UI entry point, just launches. No login, no navigation — a single command line forced entry. The important part here is that this doesn't need to come from adb at all — any other (malicious) app on the device could build the same Intent in code and call `startActivity()` and get the same result. adb is just the easiest way to simulate that.

## 4. Read what the screen's code actually does

![4](/assets/img/posts/2026-08-10/4.png)

Getting the call to land is one thing, but what matters is what the screen does, so I opened `onCreate()`. Found the real issue there: it pulls a string extra called `redirect_url`, parses it with `Intent.parseUri()` with no validation at all, and fires `startActivity()` straight off it. Below that there's even code that pulls a Parcelable Intent called `redirect_intent` and launches it directly, unchecked.

The original intent (pun intended) was probably something legitimate — connect out to an external service after scanning a QR code — but the end result is an open redirector that will execute whatever arbitrary Intent an outside caller hands it. At this point the shape of the attack was obvious: this could be used to reach screens that are `exported="false"` and normally unreachable directly.

## 5. Chain the redirect into the password-reset screen

![5](/assets/img/posts/2026-08-10/5.png)

Put an explicit Intent URI pointing at `ChangePasswordActivity` into `redirect_url` and fired again:

```
adb shell "am start -n com.sosung.test_intent/.QRConnectActivity --es redirect_url "intent:#Intent;component=com.sosung.test_intent/.ChangePasswordActivity;end""
```

Worked exactly as expected. `QRConnectActivity` took `redirect_url` and launched `ChangePasswordActivity` straight off it, and the password reset screen came up. Changed it to `4567`, and the "password changed to 4567" toast confirmed it.

This is the core of the whole thing. `ChangePasswordActivity` is clearly declared `exported="false"` in the Manifest, so the system blocks any other app from calling `am start -n .../.ChangePasswordActivity` directly. But once another `exported="true"` component forwards an Intent without validation, that protection is simply bypassed. This confirmed directly that the assumption "it's exported=false, so it's safe" can be wrong on its own.

## 6. Log in again with the new password

![6](/assets/img/posts/2026-08-10/6.png)

Logged in again with the newly-set `admin` / `4567`. No new account created, no session hijacked — just Intent chaining used to reset the admin's password to a value of my choosing.

## 7. Login succeeds — account takeover complete

![7](/assets/img/posts/2026-08-10/7.png)

Login succeeds, "thanks for logging in" message, straight into the main board. The full chain, summarized:

**Find an exported Activity → confirm unvalidated Intent Redirection inside it → chain the redirect to bypass into the exported=false password-reset screen → change the password arbitrarily → log in**

Five steps. Building this by hand, the takeaway is: declaring `exported="false"` on one screen doesn't mean it's safe. What actually matters is whether there's a reachable path from an exported component to a non-exported one, and whether unvalidated Intent forwarding sits anywhere in that path. Walking through this by hand — from force-calling a component with `am start` to tracing Intent Redirection in the source — made the line from my last post, that `exported=true` isn't the problem by itself, land a lot harder.

Next up: how to actually patch this — caller verification with `getCallingPackage()`, whitelisting which components a redirect is allowed to reach — planning to work through that hands-on next.
